1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,761
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,781 --> 00:00:08,850
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:13,502
This is Lecture 4, the thread take-up.

4
00:00:13,902 --> 00:00:18,735
The arm swinging up and down at the front of the head is the take-up lever.

5
00:00:18,755 --> 00:00:22,164
By shaft angle it lends thread out and takes it back,

6
00:00:22,184 --> 00:00:26,241
and if it gets that slightly wrong, the thread breaks or loops.

7
00:00:27,761 --> 00:00:28,461
Here's the problem.

8
00:00:28,861 --> 00:00:34,129
A lockstitch machine with this lecture's take-up dimensions,

9
00:00:34,149 --> 00:00:36,244
catching at 206 degrees,

10
00:00:36,264 --> 00:00:41,708
a 10-millimetre check-spring stroke and a 3-millimetre stitch.

11
00:00:42,108 --> 00:00:47,248
On 1.5-millimetre shirting everything is fine.

12
00:00:47,648 --> 00:00:55,068
On 3-millimetre denim the thread keeps breaking and the stitches go tight and loose.

13
00:00:55,468 --> 00:00:58,135
The line leader also asks:

14
00:00:58,155 --> 00:01:03,717
how much needle thread does a 50-centimetre seam need?

15
00:01:04,117 --> 00:01:06,119
We'll answer both at the end.

16
00:01:07,639 --> 00:01:11,299
How much thread does one stitch need?.

17
00:01:12,819 --> 00:01:14,094
First the totals.

18
00:01:14,494 --> 00:01:22,074
Only about 4.5 millimetres of needle thread actually stays in the cloth each stitch.

19
00:01:22,474 --> 00:01:26,168
Yet as the loop goes round the bobbin case,

20
00:01:26,188 --> 00:01:29,968
the needle side needs nearly 80 millimetres.

21
00:01:30,368 --> 00:01:36,015
All that has to be lent out first and taken back afterwards.

22
00:01:36,415 --> 00:01:43,676
The lever can give at most 82 millimetres per stitch, just enough.

23
00:01:44,076 --> 00:01:49,793
That's the take-up's job: lending and taking back thread by shaft angle.

24
00:01:51,313 --> 00:01:54,373
Let's follow the needle thread.

25
00:01:54,773 --> 00:01:57,657
From the cone it goes through the tension,

26
00:01:57,677 --> 00:02:02,012
two discs pressed by a spring that give the thread its tension;

27
00:02:02,032 --> 00:02:07,128
round the check spring; through a guide into the eye of the take-up lever;

28
00:02:07,148 --> 00:02:12,313
then down through more guides and the needle-bar guide into the needle eye.

29
00:02:12,713 --> 00:02:14,333
The key is the middle stretch.

30
00:02:14,733 --> 00:02:19,383
As the lever eye moves, the total length of the two spans changes.

31
00:02:19,783 --> 00:02:24,892
Longer, and thread is drawn up; shorter, and it's let down.

32
00:02:26,412 --> 00:02:32,536
This is the panel view of Lab 4-1: the four-bar take-up carrying the eye.

33
00:02:32,936 --> 00:02:37,076
Each stitch, the lever does three jobs.

34
00:02:37,476 --> 00:02:43,237
Give: as the needle goes in and the hook draws the loop bigger,

35
00:02:43,257 --> 00:02:46,816
the lever comes down paying out thread.

36
00:02:47,216 --> 00:02:51,434
Give too little and the hook has to drag it, tension jumps,

37
00:02:51,454 --> 00:02:53,230
and the thread may break.

38
00:02:53,630 --> 00:02:58,418
Take up: after the loop slips off, it rises fast,

39
00:02:58,438 --> 00:03:02,050
pulling the interlace into the cloth.

40
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:04,462
Draw: a little further up,

41
00:03:04,482 --> 00:03:09,073
drawing the next stitch's thread through the tension discs.

42
00:03:10,593 --> 00:03:12,520
Giving is the hardest job,

43
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:18,810
because the needle and hook decide how much is needed and the lever can only follow;

44
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:22,554
so the supply curve has to cover the demand curve.

45
00:03:22,954 --> 00:03:27,530
Sometimes the lever gives faster than the needle uses,

46
00:03:27,550 --> 00:03:33,148
and loose thread hanging at the head can be pierced by the needle.

47
00:03:33,547 --> 00:03:35,947
The check spring is the buffer:

48
00:03:35,967 --> 00:03:41,801
it takes up slack when the thread is loose and is pulled onto its stop when

49
00:03:41,821 --> 00:03:42,659
it's tight.

50
00:03:43,059 --> 00:03:48,048
One service manual gives about 7 millimetres of stroke,

51
00:03:48,068 --> 00:03:51,599
finished as the point enters the cloth.

52
00:03:53,119 --> 00:03:54,499
The four-bar take-up.

53
00:03:56,019 --> 00:04:02,479
Look along the main shaft from the head end, with the shaft centre as origin.

54
00:04:02,879 --> 00:04:06,659
The crank pin P comes straight from the shaft angle.

55
00:04:07,059 --> 00:04:12,227
The rocker joint M is a from P and b from the fixed pivot Q,

56
00:04:12,247 --> 00:04:16,637
so it's where two circles meet; if they don't meet,

57
00:04:16,657 --> 00:04:19,836
the linkage can't be assembled there.

58
00:04:20,236 --> 00:04:27,216
Turn PM by alpha and extend to length c, and that's the eye E.

59
00:04:27,616 --> 00:04:33,695
The transmission angle mu is best near 90 degrees;

60
00:04:33,715 --> 00:04:42,844
in our example it stays between 41 and 115 over a turn, above the usual 40.

61
00:04:44,364 --> 00:04:49,631
What matters to the thread isn't how high the eye is but the total length L

62
00:04:49,651 --> 00:04:50,829
of the two spans,

63
00:04:50,849 --> 00:04:52,944
guide to eye and eye to guide.

64
00:04:53,344 --> 00:05:00,665
In our example L is longest at 55 degrees, 210.2 millimetres,

65
00:05:00,685 --> 00:05:09,089
the tightest moment, and shortest at 293, 128.6; the difference, 81.6,

66
00:05:09,109 --> 00:05:14,144
is the most thread it can give per stitch.

67
00:05:14,544 --> 00:05:19,444
Supply S is L-max minus the current L.

68
00:05:19,844 --> 00:05:28,624
Giving takes 240 degrees and taking up only 120: slow out, fast back.

69
00:05:30,143 --> 00:05:32,683
Thread demand and surplus.

70
00:05:34,203 --> 00:05:38,320
Now the demand on the needle and hook side, D,

71
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:41,558
treating the thread as inextensible.

72
00:05:41,958 --> 00:05:43,218
Before the catch,

73
00:05:43,238 --> 00:05:49,016
once the eye is through the cloth there are two strands below, down and back,

74
00:05:49,036 --> 00:05:51,426
each as long as the eye is deep.

75
00:05:51,826 --> 00:05:55,983
So D is twice x minus e-nought, or zero.

76
00:05:56,003 --> 00:06:03,188
e-nought is how high the eye sits above the cloth at top dead centre,

77
00:06:03,208 --> 00:06:06,530
18.5 millimetres in our example.

78
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:17,589
The eye goes in at about 109 degrees, and at the bottom D is 25 millimetres.

79
00:06:19,109 --> 00:06:25,924
After the catch the thread below becomes a loop dragged by the hook point,

80
00:06:25,944 --> 00:06:27,772
a complicated shape.

81
00:06:28,172 --> 00:06:30,032
We use a simplified model.

82
00:06:30,432 --> 00:06:36,204
Looking along the hook shaft, the loop is stretched between the cloth hole,

83
00:06:36,224 --> 00:06:41,146
the hook point and the part of the case rim the point has swept,

84
00:06:41,166 --> 00:06:46,321
and the demand is the perimeter of the convex hull of those points.

85
00:06:46,721 --> 00:06:53,042
The two strands before the catch are just the hull of two points,

86
00:06:53,062 --> 00:06:54,701
so it's one idea.

87
00:06:55,101 --> 00:06:59,174
At the catch it's 21 millimetres;

88
00:06:59,194 --> 00:07:04,881
with the point at the bottom it peaks at 79.6.

89
00:07:06,401 --> 00:07:09,218
Once the loop is off the case,

90
00:07:09,238 --> 00:07:16,028
the demand falls to what the stitch actually keeps: q is about s plus t.

91
00:07:16,428 --> 00:07:20,357
The interlace is in the middle of the cloth,

92
00:07:20,377 --> 00:07:27,268
so each stitch the thread runs one stitch length along the face and goes down

93
00:07:27,288 --> 00:07:29,602
and up half the thickness,

94
00:07:29,622 --> 00:07:31,128
which adds one t.

95
00:07:31,528 --> 00:07:36,581
Here q is about 4.5 millimetres.

96
00:07:36,981 --> 00:07:44,753
Handy on the shop floor: a 50-centimetre seam is about 167 stitches,

97
00:07:44,773 --> 00:07:52,774
about 75 centimetres of needle thread, about 86 with 15 percent waste.

98
00:07:54,294 --> 00:07:59,905
Put supply and demand on one chart; the difference is the surplus.

99
00:08:00,305 --> 00:08:04,556
Positive means extra thread, taken up by the check spring;

100
00:08:04,576 --> 00:08:08,605
negative means too little, and the hook has to drag it.

101
00:08:09,005 --> 00:08:14,944
In our example the surplus at the catch is about 24 millimetres,

102
00:08:14,964 --> 00:08:19,879
the extra a four-bar gives: it is smooth and durable,

103
00:08:19,899 --> 00:08:23,882
and the price is usually some extra thread.

104
00:08:24,282 --> 00:08:30,202
With the hook point at the bottom it's smallest, only 1.9 millimetres,

105
00:08:30,222 --> 00:08:33,511
the tightest moment of the whole cycle.

106
00:08:33,911 --> 00:08:39,971
The vertical line follows the shaft; watch it sweep past that point.

107
00:08:41,491 --> 00:08:44,397
Take the three criteria in time order.

108
00:08:44,797 --> 00:08:50,197
First, before the eye enters the cloth, the needle uses no thread,

109
00:08:50,217 --> 00:08:54,549
so everything given must be held by the check spring:

110
00:08:54,569 --> 00:08:57,751
the surplus must not exceed its stroke;

111
00:08:57,771 --> 00:09:01,200
our maximum is 9.2 against a stroke of 10.

112
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:07,580
Second, from eye-in to the catch, it may be short by no more than a millimetre.

113
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:10,679
Third, and most important,

114
00:09:10,699 --> 00:09:19,465
from catch to cast-off the surplus must stay positive with 1 to 2 millimetres spare;

115
00:09:19,485 --> 00:09:20,720
ours is 1.9.

116
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:27,877
After cast-off, take it up fast and draw the next stitch's thread.

117
00:09:29,397 --> 00:09:31,064
Adjustment and trade-offs.

118
00:09:32,584 --> 00:09:37,652
On screen the cloth has gone from 1.5 to 3 millimetres,

119
00:09:37,672 --> 00:09:39,964
everything else the same.

120
00:09:40,364 --> 00:09:46,101
Thicker cloth makes the two strands under the needle longer and puts the

121
00:09:46,121 --> 00:09:48,739
cloth hole further from the case,

122
00:09:48,759 --> 00:09:51,297
so the whole demand curve rises.

123
00:09:51,697 --> 00:09:57,429
The minimum margin goes from plus 1.9 to -0.9 millimetres,

124
00:09:57,449 --> 00:10:01,395
and after the eye enters it's 1.9 short.

125
00:10:01,795 --> 00:10:08,308
The loop runs out of thread, the hook drags it out of the tension discs,

126
00:10:08,328 --> 00:10:11,575
tension leaps, and the thread snaps.

127
00:10:11,975 --> 00:10:13,558
That's why denim breaks it.

128
00:10:15,078 --> 00:10:20,120
The fix is to move the thread guide in the give-more direction;

129
00:10:20,140 --> 00:10:24,378
the manual says left for thick cloth, right for thin.

130
00:10:24,778 --> 00:10:31,796
On the right, 3-millimetre cloth with the guide moved 12 millimetres.

131
00:10:32,196 --> 00:10:39,137
The minimum margin is back to 1.4; the shortfall is down to 0.6, under one;

132
00:10:39,157 --> 00:10:43,313
and before the eye enters the surplus is 8.5,

133
00:10:43,333 --> 00:10:47,490
still inside the 10-millimetre spring stroke.

134
00:10:47,890 --> 00:10:49,950
All three criteria are met.

135
00:10:51,470 --> 00:10:54,187
Hook timing changes the demand curve too.

136
00:10:54,587 --> 00:10:57,792
With the point early, at 198 degrees,

137
00:10:57,812 --> 00:11:05,114
the extra thread round the case drops to 18 millimetres and the margin is still 1.7.

138
00:11:05,514 --> 00:11:16,254
Late, at 214, the margin is down to 0.7 while the extra rises to 31.

139
00:11:16,654 --> 00:11:22,895
So after re-timing the hook or fitting a new one, as we will in Lecture 5,

140
00:11:22,915 --> 00:11:27,549
test-sew thin and thick cloth and re-check the take-up.

141
00:11:29,069 --> 00:11:34,246
The heart of take-up design is a supply curve that covers the demand,

142
00:11:34,266 --> 00:11:37,409
with the check spring taking up the extra.

143
00:11:37,809 --> 00:11:42,669
A welding robot's cable dress faces the same problem.

144
00:11:43,069 --> 00:11:47,689
As the wrist turns the cable needs more length, then less;

145
00:11:47,709 --> 00:11:52,969
too little and it's torn, too much and it sags and snags the work.

146
00:11:53,369 --> 00:11:58,484
So a spring retractor near axes three and four takes up the slack,

147
00:11:58,504 --> 00:12:00,429
just like a check spring.

148
00:12:00,829 --> 00:12:05,864
Electronic cams on servo motors too: the steeper the curve you follow,

149
00:12:05,884 --> 00:12:09,474
the higher the acceleration and peak motor torque.

150
00:12:10,994 --> 00:12:14,560
An everyday example: flying a kite.

151
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:19,838
When it climbs you must let line out in time;

152
00:12:19,858 --> 00:12:25,171
give too little and the line goes taut and snaps.

153
00:12:25,571 --> 00:12:30,631
When the wind drops and it falls, you must reel in fast,

154
00:12:30,651 --> 00:12:33,988
or the slack line drapes into a tree.

155
00:12:34,388 --> 00:12:39,864
The reel in your hand is the take-up lever: when to give, how much,

156
00:12:39,884 --> 00:12:45,688
when to take back, all decided by what the kite needs at the other end.

157
00:12:47,208 --> 00:12:49,123
Back to the start.

158
00:12:49,523 --> 00:12:55,348
Going from 1.5 to 3-millimetre cloth lifts the demand curve;

159
00:12:55,368 --> 00:13:03,531
the minimum margin becomes -0.9 millimetres and after the eye enters it's 1.9 short,

160
00:13:03,551 --> 00:13:09,279
so the hook drags thread out of the tension, tension leaps,

161
00:13:09,299 --> 00:13:11,423
and the thread breaks.

162
00:13:11,823 --> 00:13:19,223
Move the guide about 12 millimetres toward giving more and the margin is back

163
00:13:19,243 --> 00:13:19,898
to 1.4,

164
00:13:19,918 --> 00:13:22,596
with all three criteria met.

165
00:13:22,996 --> 00:13:32,825
Thread: about 167 stitches at 4.5 millimetres, about 75 centimetres,

166
00:13:32,845 --> 00:13:36,446
86 with 15 percent waste.

167
00:13:37,966 --> 00:13:42,754
